# Point a fresh profile's chrome://flags at the defaults this browser means.
#
# The entries seeded here are not hidden or forced: every one of them appears
# in chrome://flags exactly as it would otherwise, and a user who changes one
# owns it from then on - SeedFlags() and this registration both skip entries
# already present. This decides only which way they point before anyone
# touches them.
#
# The fingerprinting group is here to close a gap between our own platforms.
# theme.sh compiles canvas image-data noise, measureText noise, client-rects
# noise and WebGL vendor/renderer spoofing into the Android build always-on,
# with no switch to turn them off, because Vanadium has no flags-seeding
# mechanism to make them optional. On desktop the same four existed as
# first-run choices, so a desktop profile whose owner closed the first-run tab
# had none of them. Same browser, same version, materially easier to
# fingerprint - decided by which build you happened to install rather than by
# anyone's intent. The desktop default now matches the phone.
#
# reduced-system-info and remove-client-hints join them for the same reason,
# arriving at the state Android is already in: hardwareConcurrency answers 2,
# the user agent reports a unified platform, and high-entropy client hints are
# not populated. On Android the first of those comes from theme.sh and the
# rest from Vanadium's kClientHintsFromReducedUA, which ships enabled there.
#
# What this costs, stated rather than implied: hardwareConcurrency answering 2
# will make a site that sizes a worker pool from it use two workers, and a
# blank WebGL renderer string will make a site that switches effects on the GPU
# name pick its fallback. Both are visible in chrome://flags and both are one
# click to undo, which is the difference between this and compiling them in.
#
# Not seeded: aerium-time-zone. It is the one mitigation whose breakage is
# silent - a wrong meeting time looks like a wrong meeting time, not like a
# browser setting - so it stays opt-in on every platform.
--- a/components/webui/flags/pref_service_flags_storage.cc
+++ b/components/webui/flags/pref_service_flags_storage.cc
@@ -87,7 +87,49 @@
 
 // static
 void PrefServiceFlagsStorage::RegisterPrefs(PrefRegistrySimple* registry) {
-  registry->RegisterListPref(prefs::kAboutFlagsEntries);
+  // Flags a fresh profile starts with. They are still ordinary chrome://flags
+  // entries - visible, listed, and switchable - this only decides which way
+  // they point before anyone touches them.
+  //
+  // The fingerprinting group below used to be here as an explicit absence,
+  // offered on the first-run page as a visible choice rather than seeded. That
+  // reasoning does not survive contact with what the Android build does: over
+  // there the same four mitigations are compiled always-on, with no switch at
+  // all. The same browser was therefore harder to fingerprint on a phone than
+  // on a desktop, for no reason anyone chose - just because Android has no
+  // flags-seeding mechanism and desktop does. Parity is the point, so the
+  // desktop default moves to match the phone.
+  base::ListValue default_flags;
+  // A security backstop - don't silently download-and-run a CRX/user-script
+  // MIME type without asking first.
+  default_flags.Append("extension-mime-request-handling@2");
+  // Parallel downloading is a plain speed win with no privacy or security
+  // trade-off to weigh, so there is nothing for a user to decide about it.
+  // The Guard presets seed it too, but only for users who pick a preset;
+  // seeding it here is what makes it the default for everyone else.
+  // SeedFlags() skips entries already present, so the two never collide.
+  // "@1" is the Enabled option: enable-parallel-downloading is a
+  // FEATURE_VALUE_TYPE entry, and FeatureEntry::DescriptionForOption() maps
+  // FEATURE_VALUE index 0/1/2 to Default/Enabled/Disabled.
+  default_flags.Append("enable-parallel-downloading@1");
+  // The fingerprinting group, matching what theme.sh compiles in on Android.
+  // The three bromite entries are SINGLE_VALUE_TYPE - a bare command-line
+  // switch, no option index - so they carry no "@" suffix; the two
+  // ungoogled-chromium ones are FEATURE_VALUE_TYPE and FEATURE_WITH_PARAMS,
+  // where "@1" is Enabled (with the feature's own defaults, which for
+  // spoof-webgl-info is the blank renderer and vendor strings).
+  default_flags.Append("fingerprinting-canvas-image-data-noise");
+  default_flags.Append("fingerprinting-canvas-measuretext-noise");
+  default_flags.Append("fingerprinting-client-rects-noise");
+  default_flags.Append("spoof-webgl-info@1");
+  // hardwareConcurrency answers 2, the user agent reports a unified platform,
+  // and only low-entropy client hints are populated. Android arrives at the
+  // same place by two other routes: theme.sh clamps hardwareConcurrency, and
+  // Vanadium's kClientHintsFromReducedUA ships enabled.
+  default_flags.Append("reduced-system-info@1");
+  default_flags.Append("remove-client-hints@1");
+  registry->RegisterListPref(prefs::kAboutFlagsEntries,
+                             std::move(default_flags));
   registry->RegisterDictionaryPref(prefs::kAboutFlagsOriginLists);
 }
 
